[{:adr/related ["90-docs/adr/2607172210-kotobase-protocol-layer-extension.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607172300-kotobase-query-protocol-extension.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607161817-kotoba-lang-dtn-tcp-transport-operational.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607162135-kotoba-lang-io-libp2p-real-tcp-transport.edn"], :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2607172600-kotobase-bitcoin-ethereum-clients", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/last_verified "2026-07-17", :adr/body "# ADR-2607172600: kotobase blockchain-client extension — Bitcoin P2P client + Ethereum JSON-RPC client, kotobase-backed\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-17\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n**Scope**: `kotoba-lang` org — 2 new repos\n**Depends on**: `kotoba-lang/kotobase` (`IStore`), the transport discipline established by\nADR-2607161817/ADR-2607162135 (`kotoba-lang/dtn`/`io-libp2p` real TCP transports) and\n`kotoba-lang/nostr`'s WebSocket work\n\n## Context\n\nThe owner asked for `bitcoin.kotobase.net` and `ethereum.kotobase.net`, initially framed the same way\nas the six prior query-protocol surfaces (WebDAV/OCI/Nostr/SFTP + Datomic/PostgreSQL/Cypher/SPARQL/\nGraphQL/Gremlin). That framing does not carry over: those six all expose **kotobase's own data**\nthrough a different wire protocol or query language. Bitcoin and Ethereum are real external networks —\nthere is no \"kotobase's own Bitcoin data\" to expose until something actually talks to those networks.\n\nClarified with the owner: build **real protocol clients** (\"kotoba 言語で bitcoin, ethereum client を\n実装\" — implement Bitcoin/Ethereum clients using this org's stack), with **kotobase as the backing\nstore** for what's observed/synced. This is a different shape of work from every prior repo in this\nproject — not a wire-protocol-in-front-of-kotobase-data problem, but a real-network-client-that-writes-\ninto-kotobase problem — and is scoped narrowly and conservatively here for a reason stated plainly:\nthis ADR does **not** authorize any transaction construction, signing, broadcasting, or private-key/\nwallet handling in either repo. Both clients are **read-only observers** of public chain data. This is\nnot a scope an owner instruction needs to re-litigate case by case — it is a standing safety boundary\nfor this class of work in this project, stated once here.\n\n**\"kotoba 言語で\" is read as \"in this org's kotoba-lang stack\"** (matching how every other repo in this\nproject — all twelve so far — was actually built: `.cljc`/`.cljs`, `kotoba-lang` org, not the literal\nminimal `.kotoba` WASM-guest dialect). This matters concretely: literal `.kotoba` WASM-guest execution\nhas no host capability for raw TCP sockets today (the same gap ADR-2607172210 already documented\nblocking SFTP's WASM-guest Phase 1) — a P2P networking client cannot run there regardless of which\nreading is intended. Both new repos follow the established family pattern: pure `.cljc` protocol-\nmessage/parsing/validation logic, `.cljc`/`.cljs`-only transport (Node `net`/`tls`/`crypto` core\nmodules, zero npm deps where practical — same discipline as `kotoba-lang/dtn`/`io-libp2p`/`nostr.relay`/\n`org-ietf-sftp`/`org-postgresql-wire`).\n\nBitcoin and Ethereum need genuinely different client shapes, not a shared template:\n\n- **Bitcoin** has no separate \"RPC protocol\" as its network-native language — the P2P wire protocol\n  (version/verack handshake, `getheaders`/`headers`, `inv`/`getdata`/`block`/`tx`) *is* how a peer talks\n  to the network. A Bitcoin \"client\" that observes chain state implements this P2P protocol directly,\n  SPV-style: sync and verify block headers (proof-of-work target check, chain linkage), optionally\n  request raw block/tx bytes for indexing. Bitcoin Core's JSON-RPC is a separate, node-operator-facing\n  admin interface, not the network protocol — implementing that instead would make this a \"remote\n  control panel for someone else's node,\" not a network client, so it is not the target here.\n- **Ethereum**'s standard client-facing interface is JSON-RPC (`eth_blockNumber`, `eth_getBlockByNumber`,\n  `eth_getBalance`, `eth_call`, `eth_getLogs`, …) — this is genuinely how the ecosystem's own tooling\n  (ethers.js, web3.js, etc.) talks to a node; implementing the underlying devp2p/RLPx encrypted P2P\n  transport instead would mean becoming a full network peer, a categorically larger and different\n  project (real peer discovery, encrypted transport handshake, sub-protocol negotiation) not justified\n  by \"have an Ethereum client.\" An Ethereum client here is a JSON-RPC client connecting outbound (HTTP or\n  WebSocket) to a real node/provider endpoint the deploy shell configures — this repo does not bundle\n  any hardcoded provider credentials.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Repo layout\n\n| Item | Name | Depends on | Shape |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bitcoin P2P client | `kotoba-lang/org-bitcoin-p2p` | `kotobase` (`IStore`) | Real P2P protocol client, SPV header sync |\n| Ethereum JSON-RPC client | `kotoba-lang/org-ethereum-jsonrpc` | `kotobase` (`IStore`) | JSON-RPC client over HTTP/WS to a configured node |\n\nNaming: both cite real, unambiguous open specs/projects — the Bitcoin P2P protocol (documented at\ndeveloper.bitcoin.org/reference/p2p_networking.html, Bitcoin Core's own reference implementation) and\nEthereum's JSON-RPC API (documented at ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/apis/json-rpc/) — matching this\nproject's `org-<body>-<spec>` discipline; no overclaiming concern here since both target real, existing,\ncitable wire specs (unlike the Datomic case).\n\n### `org-bitcoin-p2p` — v0.1 scope\n\n**In scope**: TCP connection to a configured peer (mainnet or testnet — **testnet strongly preferred\nfor any live-network testing during development**, to keep this unambiguously in \"protocol\nimplementation exercise\" territory rather than anything mainnet-value-adjacent), version/verack\nhandshake, ping/pong keepalive, `getheaders`/`headers` sync with real proof-of-work validation (hash\nmeets target, correct previous-block linkage) — this is genuine SPV trust, not a stub. Optionally\n(if time allows, not required for v0.1): `getdata`/`block`/`tx` message decoding for informational\nindexing. All synced state (headers, optionally raw block/tx bytes) persisted into `kotobase.store`\nvia `IStore`.\n\n**Explicitly, permanently out of scope for this repo** (not a v0.1-vs-v2 phasing question — a standing\nboundary): private key generation/storage, transaction construction, transaction signing, transaction\nbroadcast/relay, mempool participation, mining, full Bitcoin Script/consensus validation (script\nvalidation is exhaustively detailed and a subtly-wrong implementation is worse than an honestly-scoped\nabsence of it — this repo does header-level SPV trust, not full-node consensus verification). No\nwallet functionality of any kind.\n\nNamespace split: `kotobase.bitcoin.protocol` (pure `.cljc` — message wire-format encode/decode, header\nvalidation logic, no sockets) + `kotobase.bitcoin.transport` (`.cljs`-only, `node:net`, the actual TCP\nconnection and message framing) — same core/transport separation as every prior repo in this project.\n\n### `org-ethereum-jsonrpc` — v0.1 scope\n\n**In scope**: an HTTP and/or WebSocket JSON-RPC client (the deploy shell configures the target node/\nprovider URL — no bundled credentials) calling read-only methods: `eth_blockNumber`,\n`eth_getBlockByNumber`, `eth_getTransactionByHash`, `eth_getBalance`, `eth_call` (read-only contract\ncalls), `eth_getLogs`. Results persisted into `kotobase.store` via `IStore` as an observed-data cache.\n\n**Explicitly, permanently out of scope**: `eth_sendTransaction`/`eth_sendRawTransaction` or any method\nthat constructs or broadcasts a transaction, private key management, wallet functionality, contract\ndeployment.\n\nNamespace split: `kotobase.ethereum.rpc` (pure `.cljc` — JSON-RPC request/response shaping, method\nwhitelist enforcement, decode of returned block/tx/log data) + a thin `.cljs` HTTP/WS client wrapper\n(reuse `kotobase.protocols.json`-shaped dependency-zero JSON handling from `kotobase-protocols` where\npractical, or Node's built-in `fetch`).\n\n## Rejected\n\n- **Bitcoin Core RPC instead of P2P protocol** — would make this a remote-admin client for one specific\n  node's API, not a network client; doesn't match \"client\" in the network-protocol sense the owner\n  asked for.\n- **Ethereum devp2p/RLPx P2P client instead of JSON-RPC** — categorically larger project (real peer\n  discovery + encrypted transport + sub-protocol negotiation), not proportionate to \"have an Ethereum\n  client\" when JSON-RPC is the ecosystem's own standard client interface.\n- **Any transaction-signing/wallet capability in either repo** — explicitly and permanently rejected,\n  not a phasing decision. If a future need for this arises it requires its own dedicated ADR with its\n  own explicit owner sign-off and security review, not an incidental addition here.\n- **Literal `.kotoba` WASM-guest execution** — no host capability for raw TCP sockets exists today (same\n  gap already documented for SFTP in ADR-2607172210); both clients need real socket I/O and use the\n  established `.cljc`/`.cljs` family pattern instead.\n\n## Verification\n\nSame bar as every repo in this project: `nbb bin/run_tests.cljs` green (core `.cljc` logic JVM-testable,\n`.cljs`-only transport tests via nbb/Node), CI green, `nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs --check` clean.\nFor `org-bitcoin-p2p`, a real testnet header-sync demonstration (connect to a real testnet peer, sync\nand validate a real chain of headers) is the credible proof this is a genuine protocol implementation,\nnot a stub — matching the cross-process/real-socket verification discipline `kotoba-lang/dtn`/\n`kotoba-lang/nostr`/`org-postgresql-wire`/`org-ietf-sftp` already established. For `org-ethereum-jsonrpc`,\na real call against a real public testnet RPC endpoint (or a documented mock if no free endpoint is\nreadily available within the task's time budget — be honest about which) demonstrating a genuine\nround-trip is the equivalent proof.\n\n## References\n\n- ADR-2607172210 (protocol-layer-extension decision — naming/repo-layout discipline, and the SFTP\n  WASM-guest host-capability gap this ADR's \"kotoba 言語で\" reading note relies on)\n- ADR-2607161817, ADR-2607162135 (`kotoba-lang/dtn`/`io-libp2p` real TCP transport — the transport\n  discipline both new repos follow)\n- `kotoba-lang/nostr`'s `nostr.relay` (WebSocket transport precedent, relevant if `org-ethereum-jsonrpc`\n  uses a WS RPC endpoint)\n- `kotoba-lang/org-postgresql-wire`, `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-sftp` (the core/transport split + honest\n  partial-completion reporting discipline both new repos follow)\n", :db/id -1, :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2607172600: kotobase blockchain-client extension -- Bitcoin P2P client + Ethereum JSON-RPC client, kotobase-backed", :adr/source-format "edn"}]